**Default methods — syntax and rules:**

```java
interface AccountService {
    BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId);  // abstract — must implement

    // DEFAULT: has a body, implementing classes inherit it
    default String getFormattedBalance(String accountId) {
        BigDecimal bal = getBalance(accountId);  // can call abstract methods!
        return "Rs. " + bal.setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    }
}
// Classes that implement AccountService get getFormattedBalance() FREE — no override needed.
// But they CAN override if they want custom formatting.
```

**Why backward compatibility?** Before Java 8, `Iterable` had only `iterator()`. Adding `forEach()` as abstract would break EVERY class implementing Iterable (thousands of classes in the JDK + millions in user code). Solution:

```java
// java.lang.Iterable (Java 8 source)
public interface Iterable<T> {
    Iterator<T> iterator();  // existing abstract method

    default void forEach(Consumer<? super T> action) {  // NEW — default!
        for (T t : this) { action.accept(t); }
    }

    default Spliterator<T> spliterator() { ... }  // NEW — default!
}
// Result: ArrayList, LinkedList, HashSet, TreeSet — ALL get forEach() without code change.
```

**Key methods added via default in Java 8:**

`Iterable.forEach(Consumer)` — iterate with lambda.

`Collection.stream()` — create Stream.

`Collection.removeIf(Predicate)` — remove matching elements.

`List.sort(Comparator)` — sort in-place.

`List.replaceAll(UnaryOperator)` — transform each element.

`Map.forEach(BiConsumer)` — iterate key-value pairs.

`Map.getOrDefault(key, default)` — safe get.

`Map.putIfAbsent(key, value)` — conditional put.

`Map.computeIfAbsent(key, Function)` — lazy compute.

`Comparator.thenComparing()` — multi-key sort.

**Static methods — utility on interface:**

```java
interface CurrencyConverter {
    BigDecimal convert(BigDecimal amount);

    static CurrencyConverter usdToInr() {
        return amount -> amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("83.50"));
    }
}
// Called via: CurrencyConverter.usdToInr()
// NOT inherited by implementing classes!
// Cannot be overridden!
```

**Static methods are NOT inherited.** If class `X implements CurrencyConverter`, you CANNOT call `X.usdToInr()`. You must call `CurrencyConverter.usdToInr()`.

